"""Chapter 4 drill items — parties, pressure groups, elections and public opinion."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_4 = [
    q("A political party is best defined as", ["any group that seeks to influence government policy", "an organised group that seeks to win power by contesting elections", "a group of people with the same religion", "the body that conducts elections"], 1, "Contesting elections to win power is what makes an association a party. Influencing policy without seeking office is the pressure group's work, and conducting elections is INEC's."),
    q("Which of the following is a function of a political party?", ["Conducting the census", "Aggregating interests into a programme", "Settling election petitions", "Issuing currency"], 1, "Aggregating interests is the first of the party's functions. The census is a statistical exercise, election petitions are judicial, and currency is the central bank's."),
    q("The function of a party that involves selecting candidates and leaders is called", ["interest aggregation", "political recruitment", "political education", "interest articulation"], 1, "Recruitment — choosing who will stand and who will lead. Aggregation is combining demands into a programme, and education is explaining issues to the electorate."),
    q("A party that is small, elite-led and financed by a few wealthy supporters is called", ["a mass party", "a cadre party", "a coalition", "a pressure group"], 1, "A cadre party. A mass party has a large membership, branches and a written programme; a coalition is a temporary alliance of parties."),
    q("A mass party is distinguished from a cadre party by its", ["large membership and branch organisation", "reliance on a single leader", "refusal to contest elections", "lack of a manifesto"], 0, "Large membership, branches and a programme are the marks of a mass party. Reliance on one leader and the absence of a manifesto belong to the cadre form."),
    q("Which of the following is a merit of a two-party system?", ["It gives every small group a seat", "It offers the electorate a clear choice between alternative governments", "It prevents any party from winning a majority", "It requires coalitions after every election"], 1, "A clear choice and a clear majority are the merits claimed for two-party systems. Small-group representation and coalitions are features of multi-party systems."),
    q("A major disadvantage of a one-party system is that it", ["makes government unstable", "provides no effective opposition or choice", "produces too many coalitions", "gives small parties too much power"], 1, "No choice and no opposition: power without an alternative is the definition of the system's defect. Instability and coalitions are the vices of the multi-party form."),
    q("A party system in which several parties compete but one party wins every election is called", ["a one-party system", "a two-party system", "a dominant-party system", "a coalition system"], 2, "Dominant-party: competition is legal and real, but the result is predictable. A one-party system would make the competition illegal."),
    q("Nigeria's Fourth Republic is best described as", ["a one-party system", "a multi-party system with a dominant party", "a two-party system by law", "a no-party system"], 1, "Many parties are registered and contest, and one (the PDP until 2015, the APC since) has dominated. The 2015 alternation shows that the dominance is not absolute."),
    q("The main difference between a political party and a pressure group is that a pressure group", ["has no interest in politics", "does not seek to form a government", "cannot influence legislation", "must be registered by INEC"], 1, "The test is ambition: influence without office. A pressure group is intensely political, can influence legislation, and does not need INEC registration as a party."),
    q("The Nigeria Labour Congress is an example of", ["a promotional pressure group", "a sectional pressure group", "a political party", "an electoral commission"], 1, "A sectional group: it exists to protect the interests of its members, the workers. A promotional group promotes a cause that its members need not benefit from."),
    q("Which of the following is a method available to a pressure group?", ["Nominating candidates for election", "Lobbying legislators and organising strikes", "Conducting the census", "Declaring a law unconstitutional"], 1, "Lobbying, petitions, strikes and demonstrations are its methods. Nominating candidates is a party's, conducting a census is a statistician's, and declaring a law void is the court's."),
    q("An election is best defined as", ["a process by which the adult population chooses people to hold public office", "a debate between candidates", "the registration of political parties", "a vote in the legislature"], 0, "The process of choosing officeholders by the vote of the eligible population. A debate is part of a campaign, registration precedes it, and a legislative vote is a different act."),
    q("The franchise is", ["the right to contest an election", "the right to vote", "the body that conducts elections", "the method of counting votes"], 1, "The franchise is the right to vote. The right to stand is candidacy, the body is INEC, and the method of counting belongs to the electoral system."),
    q("In Nigeria, the right to vote is granted to every citizen who", ["owns property", "is at least eighteen and registered", "has paid tax for five years", "can read and write"], 1, "Section 77 and the Electoral Act give the vote to citizens of eighteen who are registered. Property, tax and literacy qualifications belonged to the colonial franchise."),
    q("Universal adult suffrage in Nigeria was introduced in", ["1922", "1954", "1960", "1979"], 3, "1979, with the presidential Constitution. The 1922 franchise was restricted by sex and property, and the intervening constitutions widened it step by step."),
    q("A system in which the candidate with the highest number of votes in a constituency wins is called", ["proportional representation", "first past the post", "the second ballot", "the single transferable vote"], 1, "First past the post: a plurality is enough. The second ballot requires a run-off without a majority, and proportional representation allocates seats by share of the vote."),
    q("The main advantage claimed for proportional representation is that it", ["always produces a single-party majority", "gives parties seats in proportion to their votes", "is simpler to administer than any other system", "removes the need for constituencies"], 1, "Proportionality is its purpose — fairness to small parties. Its costs are coalitions, weaker constituency links and complexity."),
    q("Which electoral system is used for elections to Nigeria's National Assembly?", ["Proportional representation", "First past the post", "The single transferable vote", "A mixed member system"], 1, "First past the post in single-member constituencies. Nigeria also uses it for the presidency, with the added requirement of a spread of votes across the states."),
    q("The body responsible for conducting federal elections in Nigeria is", ["the National Assembly", "INEC", "the Supreme Court", "the Federal Character Commission"], 1, "The Independent National Electoral Commission, established by the Constitution. It also registers parties and voters and announces results."),
    q("The independence of INEC is protected mainly by", ["its funding from the Consolidated Revenue Fund and the security of tenure of its members", "the President's control of its budget", "its subordination to the National Assembly", "the appointment of its chairman by the Chief Justice"], 0, "Money that cannot be withheld and tenure that cannot be ended at will are the guarantees. Executive budget control and legislative subordination would be the opposite."),
    q("Which of the following is an example of electoral malpractice?", ["A secret ballot", "Ballot-box snatching and stuffing", "A public count at the polling unit", "The use of an updated register"], 1, "Snatching and stuffing are the classic malpractice. Secrecy, a public count and a current register are the marks of a free election."),
    q("Gerrymandering is", ["the buying of votes", "the drawing of constituency boundaries to favour a party", "the intimidation of voters", "the stuffing of ballot boxes"], 1, "Manipulating boundaries to favour a party. Vote-buying, intimidation and stuffing are separate malpractice with their own names."),
    q("A free and fair election requires", ["a single party on the ballot", "an independent commission, a current register and a secret ballot", "the army to announce the results", "the incumbent to control the media"], 1, "Independence, a current register, secrecy and an open count are the conditions. One-party ballots, military announcements and media control are the opposites."),
    q("Public opinion is best defined as", ["the view of the government", "the sum of the views held by the adult population on a public question", "the result of the last election", "the editorial line of a newspaper"], 1, "The climate of opinion among adults on a public question. A government's view, an election result and an editorial are each evidence of it rather than the thing itself."),
    q("Which of the following is an agent of public opinion?", ["The central bank", "The mass media", "The Supreme Court", "The census board"], 1, "The media inform, educate and provide a platform for debate. The bank, the court and the statistical board perform other functions."),
    q("The mass media perform all of the following functions except", ["informing the public", "educating the electorate", "providing a platform for debate", "adjudicating election petitions"], 3, "Election petitions belong to tribunals and courts. Informing, educating and providing a platform are the media's own functions."),
    q("A political party in opposition performs the function of", ["forming the government", "criticising and checking the government", "conducting elections", "appointing judges"], 1, "The opposition criticises, proposes alternatives and holds the government to account. Forming the government is the majority party's work, and elections and appointments belong to other bodies."),
    q("One reason a multi-party system may produce unstable government is that", ["parties never disagree", "coalitions may collapse when a partner withdraws", "the electorate cannot vote", "the constitution forbids majorities"], 1, "Coalition dependence is the standard charge: a partner's withdrawal can bring the government down. Multi-party systems do not prevent voting or forbid majorities."),
    q("A party's manifesto is", ["its constitution", "the programme of policies it promises if elected", "the list of its members", "the record of its finances"], 1, "The manifesto is the programme on which it seeks a mandate. The party constitution governs its internal organisation, and membership and finance are different records."),
    q("Which of the following best explains why pressure groups are important in a democracy?", ["They conduct elections", "They give organised expression to interests between elections", "They make laws", "They appoint ministers"], 1, "Elections are periodic; interests are continuous. Pressure groups channel demands into policy between elections, which is the function the paper wants."),
    q("The single transferable vote is a form of", ["first past the post", "proportional representation", "the second ballot", "a referendum"], 1, "It is a preferential form of proportional representation, used in Ireland and Malta. The second ballot and first past the post are majority and plurality systems."),
    q("The requirement that a presidential candidate win at least a quarter of the votes in two-thirds of the states is intended to", ["reduce the cost of elections", "ensure a national spread of support", "favour small parties", "prevent the use of the secret ballot"], 1, "It forces a candidate to build support across the country rather than in one region, which is the federal logic of the rule."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a function of a political party?", ["Recruiting political leaders", "Educating the electorate", "Conducting elections", "Aggregating interests"], 2, "Conducting elections is the electoral commission's function. Parties contest elections; they do not administer them."),
    q("An opinion poll is best described as", ["a count of the votes cast", "a sample survey used to infer the views of a population", "a meeting of party leaders", "a list of registered voters"], 1, "A poll infers the whole from a sample. Its accuracy depends on the sample being random and large enough, and a published poll can itself shift opinion."),
    q("A mandate is best defined as", ["the legal right of a government to exist", "the authority a government claims from its election victory", "the oath taken by a public officer", "the power to dissolve parliament"], 1, "The claim that the electorate endorsed the programme. Legality is a separate question, and an oath and a dissolution power are instruments of office."),
    q("Which of the following would most weaken the claim that an election was free and fair?", ["A turnout of 45 per cent", "Results announced at the polling unit", "Opposition agents excluded from the count", "The use of a biometric register"], 2, "Excluding opposition agents from the count removes the guarantee that the result is real. A modest turnout, local announcement and a biometric register are all compatible with a fair election."),
    q("A bye-law made by a local government council is made under", ["the Criminal Code", "powers conferred by law on the council", "the authority of the army", "a resolution of the Senate"], 1, "A council makes bye-laws only because a law gives it the power — which is why a bye-law is delegated legislation and is void if it exceeds the power."),
    q("Which of the following statements about pressure groups is correct?", ["They nominate candidates for public office", "They may use strikes and demonstrations as well as lobbying", "They must contest elections to be recognised", "They are banned in a democracy"], 1, "Strikes, demonstrations, petitions and lobbying are all legitimate methods. Nominating candidates and contesting elections are what make an organisation a party rather than a group."),
    q("An electoral system that combines single-member constituencies with a proportional top-up is called", ["the second ballot", "a mixed member system", "the single transferable vote", "first past the post"], 1, "A mixed member system, used in Germany and New Zealand: constituency members are elected directly and list members top up each party's share so that the result is proportional."),
]
